  <h2>Two Technologies, Two Different Principles</h2>
  <p>A metal detector, as the name says, looks only for metal. Product passing through the unit travels through an electromagnetic field; when a ferrous, non-ferrous, or stainless-steel particle disturbs that field, the system generates a signal and diverts the suspect pack to the reject side with an air jet or pusher arm. It is a simple, dependable technology proven over decades — but it cannot see any non-metallic foreign material.</p>
  <p>An X-ray food inspection system, by contrast, works on the principle of density imaging. As X-rays pass through the product, part of the beam is absorbed; any object noticeably denser than the product itself shows up as a dark spot in the image. Image-processing software evaluates those spots within milliseconds and directs the suspect pack to the reject stream. That makes it possible to detect not only metal but also glass, stone, dense bone, and dense plastics.</p>
  <p>One important distinction: this comparison covers end-of-line inspection units that work on packaged product (X-ray also comes in <a href="/en/x-ray-food-inspection/bulk-product/">dedicated models</a> that sort bulk product in-line). They do not do the same job as a <a href="/en/color-sorter/">color sorter</a> (also known as an optical sorting machine), which sorts bulk product kernel by kernel at the head of the line; the two are positioned to complement each other, not to replace each other.</p>

